name: gamelife-project
description: "GameLife — projects-first \"life as a video game\" mobile app for the user + a friend; first usable version due Mon 2026-11-02"
metadata:
  node_type: memory
  type: project
  originSessionId: c5b8c72e-3cd9-4860-9dd9-e19570f1c66a
  modified: 2026-10-06T13:38:53.230Z
---

GameLife turns the user's life into a video game — "like Habitica but more advanced", for mobile, used by the user and their friend. Kicked off 2026-10-06. Deadline: a first good, usable version before the first Monday of November, i.e. **Monday 2026-11-02**.

**Why:** The user's own goal, and their first web dev project (a learning vehicle — see [[teach-dont-build]], [[user-profile]]).

**Design direction (user's DESIGN.md, 2026-10-06):** projects/goals come first (e.g. "bench press 100 kg" with a deadline); habits are a sub-branch of projects; each step done gives XP and items ("stuffs"); the app stores photos/videos (proof) to watch in-app; the character doesn't move — just "me with my stuffs". Details live in DESIGN.md (strength project: 3 lifts to 100 kg by Dec 31). Asked them to park open questions there: XP rule ambiguity (100 vs 50+100 at multiples of 10; multi-step jumps), rest days vs "gym every day" streak, stuffs, missed deadline, friend view. Recommended photos by Nov 2, videos after (size/storage).

**Teaching progress:** 2026-10-06 — Lesson 1 (HTML: tree/DOM, head/viewport, semantic tags, `python3 -m http.server`, DevTools phone mode) given; task = static `index.html` home screen (name, level, XP bar, project with 3 lifts + progress, a do-nothing button), then commit + "review". Next: CSS. Later 2026-10-06 (user wanted non-creative learning/problem-solving): JS-basics lesson artifact https://claude.ai/artifact/UwVu4gAomyrP9fc1kjC4is — C/C#→JS lessons, playground, 7 graded quests (kgToGo, percentDone, formatLevel, countSteps, totalKg, weakestLift, boss xpForProgress where the user fills in expected XP themselves). Also suggested: W3C validator on index.html (<picture> misuse, <h3> inside <button>), level-curve math, Flexbox Froggy/Grid Garden. Then: user restructured into js/ (functions.js→rules.js w/ export pending), tests/rules.test.js, package.json (was empty); user wanted to see results, so gave DOM lesson (state + render() + addEventListener, water-counter example) with task: '+5 kg bench' button updating lifts/kgToGo on the page. Next after that: localStorage (storage.js), then CSS. 2026-10-10: user felt lost on the big picture → gave ADR-001 (PWA vs RN/Flutter vs native; PWA chosen, Capacitor later), state→handler→save→render loop, file roles; user said they know nothing in JS → explained `state` from scratch (JS objects ≈ C structs, arrays, nesting, const vs mutation/references, state→render, DevTools console exercise with a book-tracker example). Their task: console exercise, then design GameLife's state shape on paper and show it. Same day: debugged their first render (null nickname element, `if=` typo + space in id, render() recursing, listener inside render) — explained causes, they fix. Then user wanted CSS to visualize the app → CSS lesson 1 (link stylesheet, selectors, box model, DevTools phone mode + live editing, Flexbox, CSS variables, XP bar = div-in-div with % width), task: paper wireframe of home screen first, then style it; timebox CSS, don't drop the JS loop/storage. Then: <img> + relative paths, <a> vs <button>, multi-page resets JS state (→ localStorage). User now has html/{projects,friends,inventory}.html, images/, shared js/app.js. 2026-10-10 lesson: create-project modal — <dialog> showModal/close, <form method="dialog"> + FormData, steps as textarea split("\n"), state.projects → array of objects, render list with createElement/textContent/replaceChildren (not innerHTML), one JS file per page (home.js/projects.js) + shared storage.js (JSON + localStorage) — examples in a book-tracker domain; they implement in 4 steps. Steps 1-3 done 2026-10-10 (projects.js: dialog, submit → push → render, renderProjects; app.js still has old string projects). Then storage lesson: localStorage = per-origin string store, JSON.stringify/parse, '[object Object]' trap, DevTools Application tab, load()/save() in storage.js with default state there, both pages use load(); gotchas: origin (127.0.0.1 vs localhost), stale saved shape after changing default state. Suggested committing first (last commit was 2026-10-07). Storage works on projects page (end of 2026-10-10; repo github.com/Arthur-Portier/Game-Life, commit 'vamos'). Roadmap given for next: A) home.js → load() + commit (keep index.html name for Pages); B) GitHub Pages deploy + install on phone; C) steps (textarea split → [{text, done}]) shown per project; D) complete a step → gainXp/level-up in rules.js (pure, test it) → save → render, XP bar on home = core loop. Then week 2: deadlines (days left), project detail, habits, items, CSS, manifest + service worker. Recommended generic step-XP for v1, kg-based XP later. 2026-10-12: user asked for the whole-project overview → gave 4-phase roadmap (A done: home.js uses load()). Wk Oct 12–18: deploy Pages, steps, complete step→XP/level, deadlines (= core loop on phone). Wk Oct 19–25: habits+streaks, items/inventory, CSS, PWA (manifest+SW), photo proof stored locally (IndexedDB). Wk Oct 26–Nov 1: online via BaaS (Supabase suggested over own Node server), accounts, sync, friend page; freeze Oct 28. Flagged ~4–5 days behind original plan; online/friend part is the one that slides past Nov 2 if needed. After v1: videos, kg-based XP, Capacitor.

**How to apply:** Ship the essential loop early, push extras later. Proposed plan for ~4 h/day: plain HTML/CSS/JS installable PWA, localStorage first (all save/load code kept in one place), GitHub Pages hosting.
- Oct 6–11: design, screens (character, projects), core loop (complete a step → XP/level, saved), deployed on phone
- Oct 12–18: habits under projects, items, deadlines + installable offline PWA = usable v1 in daily use (safety margin)
- Oct 19–25: backend — accounts + data online, friend can see you
- Oct 26–Nov 1: photo proof, friend features, polish; feature freeze Oct 28

Env note: node is not installed inside WSL (npm resolves to the Windows install under /mnt/c); install Node in WSL (e.g. nvm) when the backend phase needs it.

Dev setup recommended 2026-10-06 (user applies it themselves): VS Code opened inside WSL via the WSL extension (it had been opening `\\wsl.localhost` over UNC), Live Server for auto-reload, format on save, no GitLens, inline AI suggestions kept off. These memory files exist in two Claude Code dirs — Windows `C:\Users\porti\.claude\projects\--wsl-localhost-Ubuntu-home-arthurooo-GameLife\memory` and WSL `~/.claude/projects/-home-arthurooo-GameLife/memory` (= `/mnt/c/...` / `\\wsl.localhost\...` from the other side); when updating memory, update both.
